1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Listen to a talk in an environmental science class.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:05,900
Biomimicry is the human practice of learning from

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,100
and imitating things found in nature in order to create new inventions.

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:17,020
This way, scientists and engineers can create innovative solutions that are both efficient and sustainable.

5
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:22,789
One well-known example of biomimicry is the development of a widely used fastening system.

6
00:00:22,789 --> 00:00:30,090
The inventor was inspired by the way burrs, uh thorns, from plants stuck to his dog's fur.

7
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:34,760
By replicating the tiny hooks found on the burrs he removed from the dog fur,

8
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:37,280
he created the new fastening system.

9
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:45,000
Another example is the design of energy-efficient buildings based on the ventilation system of structures created by termites.

10
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,180
Termites are ground-dwelling insects that use different materials to create hills,

11
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,560
or mounds, in which the termites live.

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,000
Termite mounds have a complex network of vents.

13
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:59,040
The vents maintain a stable internal temperature

14
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:01,740
which can be emulated in human architecture.

15
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:05,200
Biomimicry can also be applied to renewable energy.

16
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:11,600
For instance, researchers are developing wind turbine blades modeled after the flippers of humpback whales.

17
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:16,360
The unique shape of the flippers reduces drag and increases efficiency,

18
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,800
leading to more effective wind energy capture.
